# ACTA -- public repository.
#
# Generated as part of the release build; edit it in the source repository, not here.
#
# THE PUBLIC REPO IS FLAT -- the pipeline files sit at the root, with no version folder -- so every
# rule anchored "one level deep" upstream is ALSO anchored at the root here. Without that change a
# user's own `Titration_FCS/*.wsp` and `*.csv` would be COMMITTABLE, because
# `*/Titration_FCS/*` cannot match a path with no leading directory. Your own flow data should
# never be committable by accident.
#
# === R workspace & session artifacts (regenerable, large) ===
.RData
.RData*
.RDataTmp
.Rhistory
.Rapp.history
.Rproj.user/

# === Raw / large data — do NOT commit to external hosting ===
# Flow cytometry raw data (proprietary experimental data, large binaries)
*.fcs
*.fcs.gz
# ...EXCEPT the sanitised diagnostic data, which is part of the tool and MUST ship: without it a
# fresh clone has no test data, `actaOQRun()` cannot run, and the OQ cases are also the only worked
# examples a new user gets. Re-included explicitly so `git add -A` picks them up.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: they were force-added long ago, `*.fcs` above quietly caught them on every
# subsequent bump, and a plain `git add -A <new version>` therefore staged a version folder with
# ZERO test data -- 41 files instead of 88, which passes every local suite because the files are on
# disk, and fails from a fresh clone. This rule removes the need for a `git add -f`.
!**/Diagnostics/OQ_Test*/Titration_FCS/**
# AND THE PACKAGE LAYOUT, where the same cases sit at inst/extdata/oq_small/ instead of
# Diagnostics/. The rule above is anchored on a directory NAME, so a package release drops the
# sanitised FCS straight back under `*.fcs`. Verified against a synthetic 3.0 tree: 1 of 1 test FCS
# was DROPPED before this line existed -- the fourth time this exact anchoring failure has bitten.
!**/oq_small/OQ_Test*/Titration_FCS/**

# Large generated reports (regenerable from .Rmd)
*.pdf

# knitr / rmarkdown intermediates. render(clean = FALSE) keeps these so a FAILED render leaves the
# LaTeX .log behind to diagnose from -- which means they now appear routinely and must be ignored.
# ACTA_Report_*.knit.md had already been committed once by an over-broad `git add -A`.
*.knit.md
*.utf8.md
*.aux
# The LaTeX .log is the one intermediate that is USEFUL after a failure -- and the one that must not
# be committed. A tracked log from a failed 2_87 run carried the operator's absolute home directory
# on EVERY line. It sits at the case ROOT, not under Outputs/, so the **/OQ_Test*/Outputs/ rule
# never caught it. Read it, diagnose from it, then leave it out.
#
# Keep this note. `*.log` sitting between `*.aux` and `*.toc` reads as an ordinary build artefact,
# so a later tidy-up removes it as redundant -- and the disclosure it prevents is invisible from the
# rule alone.
*.log
*.tex
*.toc
*.out
*_files/

# Generated plot images (regenerable from scripts) — keep repo code-only
*.png

# ...except the README's own figures, which are SOURCE, not output. The negation must sit BELOW the
# rule it overrides: git applies the LAST matching pattern, so a negation placed above `*.png` is
# dead text. Without this the repo publishes a README with two broken images.
!assets/*.png

# Whole Plots/ output directory at any depth (belt-and-braces alongside *.png)
Plots/

# Generated titration exports. Regenerable by re-running the script, and they carry
# experiment metadata (ELN IDs, reagent lots, donor material) plus derived Stain Index
# values, so they are kept out of remote hosting.
*TitrationExport*.xlsx
# The per-population StatsExport is the same kind of thing.
*StatsExport*.xlsx

# WORKING flow data folder. `*.fcs` above already excludes the FILES; this excludes everything else
# a version's own working folder might collect (a vendor .csv, a FlowJo workspace, a screenshot), so
# nothing in there can be committed by accident.
#
# The CONTENTS are excluded, not the folder itself, and its README is re-included -- so the folder
# EXISTS in a fresh clone, telling a user where the FCS go, instead of having to be created by hand
# before the first run. Excluding the directory outright would make that impossible: git cannot
# re-include a path underneath an excluded directory.
#
# Anchored ONE level deep, which is exactly where a version's working folder sits: `*` does not match
# a slash, so this catches `<version>/Titration_FCS/<file>` and nothing deeper. The sanitised OQ data
# at */Diagnostics/OQ_Test*/Titration_FCS/ is four levels down and is NOT caught BY THIS RULE --
# but it WAS caught by the global `*.fcs` at the top of this file, which this comment used to deny.
# That is now handled by an explicit re-include up there; see it for what the denial cost.
Titration_FCS/*
!Titration_FCS/README.md
*/Titration_FCS/*
!*/Titration_FCS/README.md

# === WORKING instructions / layout workbooks: never upload, any version ===
# Superseded the earlier note here, which said these were "deliberately NOT ignored -- they are
# inputs the pipeline needs, not outputs". Being an input is not the test: a working workbook carries
# the RUN's own metadata -- ELN ID, operator, vendor / catalogue / lot numbers, test material and the
# plate map -- so it is proprietary regardless of the fact that the tool reading it is not. What gets
# published is the TOOL, its TESTS and its DIAGNOSTICS.
#
# Two kinds are therefore still tracked, both by design:
#   Template/<...>.xlsx              the blank template, which IS part of the tool
#   Diagnostics/*/<...>.xlsx         sanitised OQ copies, without which the OQ cases cannot run
# A working copy of the instructions workbook holds real run data, so it is never committed; the
# blank template and the sanitised OQ copies above are part of the tool and are.
# THIS TREE IS FLAT, WHICH CHANGES WHERE THE RULE BITES. Here `Template/` is one level deep and a
# working copy sits at the ROOT -- so a naive `*/*` rule ignored the blank template, which every
# user copies and
# which the comment above calls part of the tool, while leaving a root-level working workbook
# perfectly committable. Both halves are wrong, and the second is a disclosure risk.
#
# So: catch the root as well, and un-ignore the two that ship. Negations come AFTER, because git
# applies the last matching pattern. (`Diagnostics/*/` is two levels and never matched `*/*`
# anyway; it is stated so the intent survives the next person to edit this file.)
/*_Ab_Titration_Layout*.xlsx
/*_Ab_Titration_Instructions*.xlsx
*/*_Ab_Titration_Layout*.xlsx
*/*_Ab_Titration_Instructions*.xlsx
!Template/*_Ab_Titration_Instructions*.xlsx
!Diagnostics/*/*_Ab_Titration_Instructions*.xlsx

# The GENERATED dashboard. Named distinctly from the Template/*.html sources so those stay tracked.
# It had been committed into two version folders by an over-broad `git add -A`, which is also how it
# came to be duplicated into the new version by the bump.
ACTA_Titration_Dashboard*.html

# === OS / editor cruft ===
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# === Backups ===
*.bak
*.bak_*
# Layout workbooks kept as safety copies before a schema edit. The layouts themselves are
# inputs and ARE tracked, so these have to be excluded by the *_backup suffix, not by extension.
*_backup.xlsx
Archive/

# regression snapshots are run artefacts
*.rds
.acta_run_result.rds
ACTA_app_run_log.tsv
# OQ run artefacts. `**/` because the cases live at 2_87_WIP/Diagnostics/OQ_TestN/Outputs/ --
# a bare `*/` matches exactly ONE directory level, so the old pattern never fired at that depth
# and two artefacts were committed. They matter: the report and dashboard embed the ABSOLUTE
# path of whoever ran them and log.txt records the machine name, so in a folder that is meant to
# be publicly shareable these are a disclosure, not just clutter.
**/OQ_Test*/Outputs/

# THE RUN PROVENANCE PAIR, which sits at the RUN ROOT and not under Outputs/. Since 3.0.12
# run_acta() writes these on every run, so they land beside the export -- and for a diagnostic
# run that is the CASE root, which the **/OQ_Test*/Outputs/ rule above does not reach. Exactly
# the gap that let a 2_87 LaTeX log carrying an operator's absolute home directory be committed,
# noted a hundred lines up in this same file. code_dir abbreviates the home prefix, so the
# content is no longer a disclosure on its own; these stay ignored because they are regenerated
# output that nobody should be committing either way.
**/acta_version.tsv
**/package_versions.tsv
# validated_packages.tsv is the OQ BASELINE -- an input, tracked on purpose -- and is a different
# filename, so no negation is needed. Stated because the three look alike at a glance.

# The OQ compensation matrix is an INPUT the diagnostic needs, exactly like the layout workbooks.
# It is negated EXPLICITLY because it is not this file that excludes it: a user-global
# core.excludesFile carrying `*.csv` does, silently. Without this line a fresh clone gets no
# matrix, OQ_Test2's csv pre-flight reports "0 candidates", and the case looks broken rather than
# incompletely cloned. A repo .gitignore outranks the global excludes file, so this negation wins.
!**/OQ_Test*/*CompMatrix*.csv
# the OQ FCS are sanitised and downsampled, so they DO belong in git (force-added)
.acta_oq_result.rds

# The scrub token sidecar: the list of identifiers to remove IS the identifiers, so it must never
# be committed. verify_oq_sanitised.R and build_oq_test.R load it from beside themselves and run in
# a loudly-announced reduced mode when it is absent -- which is what a public clone looks like.
**/tests/.acta_scrub_tokens.R
# ...and wherever else the gates live. Under the package layout they are at tests/legacy/, which the
# rule above does not match, so the PRIVATE token list became committable in the public repo. It is
# kept out of the release today only because it is untracked in the development repo -- one layer
# where there should be two. (R CMD build needs its own guard for the same file; see .Rbuildignore.)
**/tests/**/.acta_scrub_tokens.R

# Local planning notes. Kept beside the code they describe, deliberately not tracked.
Plans/

# The build's own provenance note: meaningful only in the source repository. Stays out of this one.
RELEASE_MANIFEST.txt
